    "problem_statement": "flatten() function returns incorrect results when levels=0\n\n#### Description\n\nThe `flatten()` function is not behaving correctly when `levels=0` is passed. Instead of returning the original target unchanged, it appears to be applying some transformation.\n\n#### Steps/Code to Reproduce\n\n```python\nfrom glom.reduction import flatten\n\n# This should return the original target unchanged\ntarget = [[[1]], [[2]], [[3, 4]]]\nresult = flatten(target, levels=0)\nprint(\"Expected:\", target)\nprint(\"Actual:\", result)\n```\n\nThe expected behavior is that when `levels=0`, the function should return the target as-is without any flattening operations. However, the current implementation seems to be applying some processing even when no levels of flattening are requested.\n\nThis affects any code that relies on `levels=0` as a way to conditionally disable flattening while keeping the same function call structure."
  }
